package cel

// The reasons and sources the registry below refers to. Each describes one
// family of differences; the entries pin the individual outcomes.
const (
	parityWhyCompileNotDeny   = "The apiserver compiles the policy's expressions itself, and an expression that does not compile becomes an error decision, which failurePolicy Fail turns into a denial. The offline evaluator treats a compile failure as an offline-only failure, not an expression error, so the result is an unknown verdict and failurePolicy does not apply."
	parityWhereCompileNotDeny = "evaluator.go applyFailurePolicy and dispatch.go matchConditionsGate; apiserver plugin/cel/activation.go Evaluate (a compilation error becomes an EvaluationResult error) and policy/validating/validator.go (policyDecisionActionForError)"

	parityWhyCompileKind   = "Both sides refuse the expression and, under failurePolicy Fail, deny; under Ignore both admit. They find the problem at different times: the apiserver's compile step knows the types of request, namespaceObject and variables, requires a bool (or string) result and rejects invalid literal arguments to the IP, CIDR, quantity, semver and URL functions, while the offline environment declares object, params, request and variables as dyn and fails when the expression runs."
	parityWhereCompileKind = "env.go newEnv (dyn declarations); apiserver plugin/cel/compile.go mustBuildEnvs, BuildRequestType, BuildNamespaceType and CompileCELExpression"

	parityWhyAuthorizer   = "authorizer is deliberately not declared offline: it cannot be resolved without a cluster, so a policy that uses it fails to compile and is reported unknown. The apiserver always supplies an authorizer; the harness gives the upstream side an allow-all one."
	parityWhereAuthorizer = "env.go newEnv (authorizer is deliberately NOT declared); apiserver plugin/cel/compile.go mustBuildEnvs (HasAuthorizer)"

	parityWhyLaxStatic   = "The apiserver refuses this expression at compile time: a dyn result is not a bool or string, a variable is used before it is defined, params is used without a paramKind, a typed field is compared with null, or a field the declared type does not have is read. The offline evaluator has no static result typing and resolves variables by name, so it evaluates the expression and can reach a different verdict (here, a pass or a different message). A stored policy that the apiserver accepted never contains such an expression; whether the offline engine should refuse them too is a maintainer decision."
	parityWhereLaxStatic = "env.go newEnv (object, params, request and variables are dyn); evaluator.go lazyVariables; apiserver plugin/cel/composition.go (variables compile in order) and compile.go (ReturnTypes check)"

	parityWhyUID   = "The stub exposes request.uid, but the apiserver's request type declares no uid, so a policy that reads it does not compile there. TestStubRequestHasFullAdmissionRequestShape says the stub stays aligned with BuildRequestType and yet lists uid."
	parityWhereUID = "stub.go stubRequest; stub_test.go TestStubRequestHasFullAdmissionRequestShape; apiserver plugin/cel/compile.go BuildRequestType"

	parityWhyEmptyRequest   = "The apiserver builds request from a typed AdmissionRequest and omits fields whose value is empty, so selecting subResource, requestSubResource or the namespace of a cluster-scoped resource is an error and has() is false. The stub fills every field with a zero value so that selection never errors; for an empty value that hides an error the cluster produces, which failurePolicy Fail turns into a denial."
	parityWhereEmptyRequest = "stub.go stubRequest (fields are present-but-zero so selection never errors); apiserver plugin/cel/condition.go CreateAdmissionRequest and activation.go newActivation (SchemalessTypedToVal)"

	parityWhyIdentity   = "A scan has no requesting identity, so the stub carries an empty userInfo with every field present. The apiserver omits empty fields from request.userInfo and a real request always has a username, so a policy that reads the identity cannot be given equivalent input."
	parityWhereIdentity = "stub.go stubRequest (empty UserInfo carries no identity); apiserver plugin/cel/condition.go CreateAdmissionRequest"

	parityWhyOperation   = "The offline engine models every resource as a fresh CREATE of the main resource, with no old object and no subresource, so an UPDATE or subresource request cannot be given to it in equivalent form."
	parityWhereOperation = "stub.go operationCreate and stubBindings (oldObject is null)"

	parityWhyBudgetCollapse   = "When a validation or variable exhausts the shared cost budget, the apiserver stops the policy and returns one error decision. The offline evaluator keeps the verdicts already reached and marks the unreached validations out of budget, which the scanner reports as unknown rather than as a denial."
	parityWhereBudgetCollapse = "evaluator.go EvaluateOnObject (doc: we KEEP the verdicts already reached) and budget.go; apiserver plugin/cel/activation.go Evaluate (out of budget ends the policy) and policy/validating/validator.go"

	parityWhyBudgetIgnore   = "Under failurePolicy Ignore the apiserver admits the request once the budget is exhausted, discarding a denial reached earlier. The offline evaluator keeps that denial, so the scan reports a violation the cluster would have admitted."
	parityWhereBudgetIgnore = "evaluator.go EvaluateOnObject (doc: we KEEP the verdicts already reached); apiserver policy/validating/validator.go"

	parityWhyBudgetMessage   = "A messageExpression that exhausts the budget the validations left turns the whole validation into an error decision upstream (messageFilter.ForInput returns ErrOutOfBudget, a denial under failurePolicy Fail). Offline it only costs the message: the violation stands with its fallback text."
	parityWhereBudgetMessage = "evaluator.go resolveMessage (a failing messageExpression must NOT turn the violation into an error); apiserver policy/validating/validator.go (failed messageExpression)"

	parityWhyGateBudget   = "matchConditions that run past their own 2.5M budget fail the matcher as a whole: the apiserver denies the request under failurePolicy Fail and skips the policy under Ignore, and the budget error is raised before any condition result is read, so a false condition ahead of the exhaustion does not skip the policy. The offline gate reports the exhausted budget as an offline-only failure that failurePolicy does not apply to: the scan gets an unknown verdict, which is neither a denial nor a skip."
	parityWhereGateBudget = "dispatch.go matchConditionsGate (an exhausted budget is not an expression error, so failurePolicy does not apply) and matchconditions.go matchConditionsBudgetLimit; apiserver webhook/matchconditions/matcher.go Match (a ForInput error follows failurePolicy) and plugin/cel/condition.go ForInput (an out-of-budget error discards every result)"

	parityWhyCancel   = "A cancelled context is not a comparable request. The apiserver still finishes cheap expressions, because cel-go only checks for interruption every few steps, while the offline evaluator stops before running and reports an offline-only failure so that Ctrl+C ends a scan."
	parityWhereCancel = "evaluator.go EvaluateOnObject (a cancelled scan stops here) and matchconditions.go matchConditionsHold; apiserver plugin/cel/activation.go Evaluate"

	parityWhyZeroStruct   = "A typed Go struct always serializes a non-pointer struct field (Pod status, container resources, Namespace spec and status), so the object the apiserver hands a policy carries it as {} even when the manifest left it out. The offline object keeps the manifest as written, so has() is false and selecting into it errors. normalizeNullFields only rewrites explicit nulls."
	parityWhereZeroStruct = "nullfields.go normalizeNullFields (explicit nulls only); typedDecode in nullfields_test.go; apiserver admission.VersionedAttributes conversion of the typed object"

	parityWhyOmitEmpty   = "A typed Go struct drops an empty list or map that carries omitempty, so the object the apiserver hands a policy has no such key. The offline object keeps the empty collection, so has() is true and the collection can be indexed."
	parityWhereOmitEmpty = "nullfields.go normalizeNullFields (explicit nulls only); typedDecode in nullfields_test.go"

	parityWhyUnknownField   = "The typed decode drops a field the Go type does not declare, so a policy never sees one. The offline object keeps it, because normalizeNullFields leaves undeclared fields as written."
	parityWhereUnknownField = "nullfields.go normalizeNullFields (fields the Go type does not declare are left as written)"

	parityWhyZeroTimestamp   = "A creationTimestamp written as the zero time (0001-01-01T00:00:00Z) decodes to the zero metav1.Time, which omitzero drops from the typed object. normalizeNullFields only rewrites nulls and leaves the value of a type that decodes its own JSON as written, so the offline object keeps the string. The null normalization handles the null form only and does not change this. Whether a zero timestamp string should be treated like a null is a decision for a maintainer."
	parityWhereZeroTimestamp = "nullfields.go normalizeNulls (decodesItsOwnJSON values are left as written); apimachinery meta/v1/time.go Time.UnmarshalJSON; runtime/converter.go (omitzero fields are skipped)"
)

// parityExceptions is the registry of known differences between the offline
